  "grammar_points": [
    {
      "name": "命令形 (meireikei) — imperative form",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works:\nThe imperative form is used for direct, strong commands. For godan (u-verb) verbs, change the final -u to -e: 出す (dasu) → 出せ (dase). For ichidan (ru-verb) verbs, drop る (ru) and add ろ (ro): 食べる (taberu) → 食べろ (tabero). This form sounds forceful and is common in anime, sports, and song lyrics. It is too blunt for polite conversation.\n\nExample from this song:\n曝け出せ (sarake dase) — \"lay bare!\" / \"expose everything!\"\nA rallying cry to hold nothing back.\n\nMore examples:\n• 走れ (hashire) — \"run!\"\n• 立て (tate) — \"stand up!\"\n• 諦めるな (akirameru na) — \"don't give up!\" (negative imperative with な)"
      },
      "conjugation_path": "曝け出す (sarake dasu, \"to expose/lay bare\") → 曝け出せ (sarake dase, imperative, \"lay bare!\")"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜てゆく / 〜ていく (te yuku / te iku) — gradual change into the future",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works:\nThe pattern Verb て-form (te-form) + いく (iku) or ゆく (yuku) expresses an action or change that continues moving forward into the future. Think of it as \"going on doing ~\" or \"continuing to ~.\" The form ゆく (yuku) is more literary and poetic; いく (iku) is standard. In casual speech, ていく (te iku) contracts to てく (teku).\n\nExample from this song:\n変えて行く (kaete iku) — \"will keep changing\" / \"will go on changing\"\nThe singer declares they will keep transforming things going forward.\n\nMore examples:\n• 強くなっていく (tsuyoku natte iku) — \"keep getting stronger\"\n• 忘れていく (wasurete iku) — \"gradually forgetting\"\n• 夢を叶えていく (yume o kanaete iku) — \"keep making dreams come true\""
      },
      "conjugation_path": "変える (kaeru, \"to change\") → 変えて (kaete, te-form) → 変えて行く (kaete iku, \"will keep changing\")"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜すら (sura) — even (emphatic)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N2",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works:\nThe particle すら (sura) means \"even\" and is more emphatic and literary than さえ (sae). It highlights an extreme or unexpected example to make a stronger point. The pattern is Noun + すら (sura). If even this extreme case is true, then everything else certainly is too. It is often found in written Japanese and song lyrics.\n\nExample from this song:\n忘れた事すら (wasureta koto sura) — \"even the fact of forgetting\"\nThe singer emphasizes that even the act of forgetting itself has significance.\n\nMore examples:\n• 自分の名前すら忘れた (jibun no namae sura wasureta) — \"I forgot even my own name\"\n• 彼女は挨拶すらしなかった (kanojo wa aisatsu sura shinakatta) — \"she didn't even greet me\"\n• 一円すら持っていない (ichi-en sura motte inai) — \"I don't have even one yen\""
      },
      "conjugation_path": "忘れた事 (wasureta koto, \"the fact of forgetting\") + すら (sura, \"even\") → 忘れた事すら (wasureta koto sura, \"even the fact of forgetting\")"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜かけた (kaketa) — on the verge of / half-done",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works:\nThe pattern Verb ます-stem (masu-stem) + かける (kakeru) means \"to be on the verge of doing ~\" or \"to have started doing ~ but not finished.\" In the past tense: かけた (kaketa). It captures the moment just before something happens, or an action that was begun but interrupted.\n\nExample from this song:\n崩れかけた (kuzure kaketa) — \"was about to collapse\" / \"half-collapsed\"\nSomething was on the brink of falling apart.\n\nMore examples:\n• 死にかけた (shini kaketa) — \"nearly died\"\n• 言いかけてやめた (ii kakete yameta) — \"started to say something but stopped\"\n• 食べかけのりんご (tabe kake no ringo) — \"a half-eaten apple\""
      },
      "conjugation_path": "崩れる (kuzureru, \"to collapse\") → 崩れ (kuzure, masu-stem) → 崩れかけた (kuzure kaketa, \"was about to collapse\")"
    },
    {
      "name": "可能形 (kanoukei) — potential form 〜える/〜られる",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works:\nThe potential form expresses ability — \"can do ~\" or \"be able to ~.\" For godan (u-verb) verbs: change the final -u to -e and add る (ru): 会う (au) → 会える (aeru). For ichidan (ru-verb) verbs: drop る (ru) and add られる (rareru): 食べる (taberu) → 食べられる (taberareru). In casual speech, ichidan verbs often drop the ら (ra): 食べれる (tabereru).\n\nExample from this song:\n出会えた (deaeta) — \"was able to meet\"\nThe singer expresses gratitude for having been able to encounter someone special.\n\nMore examples:\n• 日本語が話せる (nihongo ga hanaseru) — \"I can speak Japanese\"\n• 一人で行ける (hitori de ikeru) — \"I can go alone\"\n• 信じられない (shinjirarenai) — \"I can't believe it\""
      },
